target: internal/record/record.go
verify: go test ./internal/record/ -count=1 -run 'TestLoadsStructuralRulesThatNothingElseAsserts'
expect: must be ignored, as the Python's merge effectively does
why:    the OVER-FIRING half. The folded-key check is per phase, against the names that phase's struct actually decodes, because `Agents` on a PLAN row folds onto nothing Plan reads and the Python ignores it too — refusing it would redden a healthy run for a non-reason. Checking every modelled name on every row is the tempting simplification and it is wrong in exactly that way. (Re-anchored 2026-10-09: the report slice added five names to the plan call, so the mutation now appends the finish-owned names to the widened list rather than replacing a two-name one.)
--- old
			if err := foldedKeys([]byte(line), "agent_cap", "gates", "orchestrator_model",
--- new
			if err := foldedKeys([]byte(line), "agent_cap", "gates", "agents", "applied", "asked", "unresolved_asks", "executed", "orchestrator_model",
